From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ep9
Subject: [PATCH] oxc_parser: an opt-in recursion check in ParserConfig

A deep input (`[[[[...]]]]`, `!!!!...x`, `{{{{...}}}}`, `<a><a>...`)
makes the recursive-descent parser recurse once per level, and a deep
enough input ends the process with a native stack overflow. A caller
cannot stop that from outside the parse (issue oxc-project/oxc#21953).

This adds a check that a config can turn on, with no cost when it is
off:

- `ParserConfig::CHECKS_RECURSION` (default `false`) and
  `ParserConfig::may_recurse(&self) -> bool` (default `true`). The
  built-in configs keep the defaults, so every site compiles away for
  them. A config that sets `CHECKS_RECURSION = true` gives the body,
  for example "the stack pointer is above a limit".
- 23 sites, one at each recursion entry that the call graph needs (17
  token-driven: assignment expression, unary, update, await, binary
  rest, private-in, primary (after the identifier fast path),
  statement list item, binding pattern, TS type, non-array type, type
  operator, `is_start_of_type`, type assertion, namespace declaration,
  JSX element, JSX fragment; 6 walks over a built subtree:
  `AssignmentTarget::cover`, `SimpleAssignmentTarget::cover`, the 2
  checks of a cover list at `=>` (`check_cover_binding`,
  `check_cover_binding_target`) and the 2 conversions after them
  (`cover_expression_to_binding_pattern`,
  `cover_assignment_target_to_binding_pattern`)). A site is
  `if C::CHECKS_RECURSION && !self.config.may_recurse() { return
  self.recursion_stopped(); }`; `recursion_stopped` sets a fatal
  error, as `fatal_error` does. The 2 checks take `&self` and return
  their error: there the site is `{ return
  Err(self.recursion_stopped_error()); }`, which sets the stop flag
  and gives the error to the caller.
- 2 walks become loops (`set_pure_on_call_or_new_expr` down a member
  chain, `jsx_member_expression_eq` down a JSX name chain): a member
  chain is built by a loop, so it must use no stack per member.
- The stop is sticky: `rewind` restores the fatal error of its
  checkpoint and so can drop the stop. `ParserImpl::stack_limit_hit`
  (a `Cell<bool>`, not in the checkpoint) keeps it, and the exits of
  `parse` and `parse_expression` read it: then the result is one
  "Maximum call stack size exceeded" error, a program with no body,
  `fatal_error = true`, and the new `ParserReturn::stack_limit_hit`
  (the struct is `#[non_exhaustive]`).

The check does not bound the regular expression parser when
`ParseOptions::parse_regular_expression` is on; that needs 2 more
sites in oxc_regular_expression.

Test: tests/recursion_check.rs (a deep input of each form stops with
one error; a stop at any one entry survives every rewind; a stop at
any entry of an arrow head, in the list, in a check or conversion
walk or in the body, stops the parse; a config that checks and goes
on gives the result of one that does not; a lexer error before the
stop; the 2 loops on a 1 MiB thread).

Upstream: issue oxc-project/oxc#21953; the PR text draft is
docs/upstream/oxc-parser-recursion-check.md of the ep9 repository
(not sent).
